    "lawCode": "Amd §163, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4856\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law, in relation to authorizing\nstate agencies to use discretionary purchasing authority for\nprocurements from service-disabled veteran-owned business\n\nPurpose of Bill:\n\nTo encourage State agencies to purchase commodities and services from\nservice-disabled veteran-owned businesses, this bill would amend the\nState Finance Law (i) to allow State agencies to identify a\nquantitative factor for service-disabled veteran-owned businesses in\nbest value procurement evaluations and (ii) to permit State agencies\nto make discretionary purchases of up to $200,000 from such\nbusinesses.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend State Finance Law § 163(1)(j) to\npermit the use of a quantitative factor in best value procurement\nevaluations for participation by service-disabled veteran-owned\nbusinesses (\"SDVOBs\"). Section 2 of this bill would amend State\nFinance Law § 163(6) to broaden the $200,000 discretionary purchasing\nauthority of State agencies to include purchases of commodities and\nservices from SDVOBs.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nState Finance Law § 163(1)(j) permits the use of a quantitative factor\nin best value procurements relating to recognizing participation by\nminority- and women-owned businesses (\"MWBEs\") and small businesses\nState Finance Law § 163(6) currently provides State agencies with\n$200,000 of discretionary purchasing authority for procurements\nwithout a formal competitive process from MWBEs and small businesses.\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nNone\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nChapter 22 of the Laws of 2014 enacted a new Article 17-B of the\nExecutive Law, which creates a new Division of Service-Disabled\nVeteran-Owned Business Development in the Office of General Services.\nThe Division is empowered to certify qualified SDVOBs and to increase\nopportunities for such certified businesses to have more opportunities\nto participate in public procurements. Executive Law § 369-j(c)\nestablishes a Statewide goal of six percent for participation of\ncertified SDVOBs in State procurement contracts.\n\nThe definition of the term \"best value\" in State Finance Law\n163(I)(j) currently permits State agencies to use a quantitative\nfactor to encourage the participation of small businesses and MWBEs in\nbest value procurements This bill would permit State agencies to use a\nquantitative factor for SDVOBs. Pursuant to State Finance Law\n\n\n163(6), State agencies have discretion to purchase up to $200,000 of\ncommodities, services and technology from MWBEs and small businesses.\nThis bill would allow discretionary purchases of up to $200,000 from\nSDVOBs.\n\nThe use of quantitative factors in best value procurements and the use\nof discretionary purchasing have been valuable tools for State\nagencies in meeting their MWBE participation goals. Facilitating\nparticipation by SDVOBs through the use of a quantitative factor in\nbest value procurements and broadening the discretionary purchasing\nauthority to include SDVOBs would similarly help State agencies meet\ntheir SDVOB participation goals and would further the aims of this new\nprogram.\n\nBudget Implications:\n\nThis bill would have no fiscal implications for the State.\n\nLocal Impact:\n\nNot known.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
